#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203702 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04206f72-e018-5925-a58f-179e897595dc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Existence of a Multiplicative Inverse"}] :question/id #uuid "b0bec0e5-41ab-591f-ab80-5e43602ff808" :question/math-academy-id "e-14656" :question/problem "Which of the following integers has a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$?\n\n1. $7$\n2. $10$\n3. $12$" :question/worked-solution "An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(a,n)=1\n$$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- $7$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(7,25)=1. \\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n- $10$ does *not* have a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(10,25)=5\\neq1. \\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n- $12$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(12,25)=1. \\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203703 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d467de67-1ec6-57ab-98e7-8f7e9797d54d" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Inverses Using Exponentiation"}] :question/id #uuid "060283e9-0fbd-523b-bc30-37936fabacf5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14657" :question/problem "Use trial and error to compute the multiplicative inverse of $8$ modulo $15$." :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative inverse of $8$ modulo $15$, denoted $8^{-1}$, is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n8 \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:15)\n$$\n\nNote that since\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(8,15) = 1\n$$\n\nan inverse element exists and is unique up to congruence modulo $15$.\n\nSince the modulus $n=15$ is small, we can find the inverse $x$ by computing exponents of $8$ (modulo $15$) until we get a result of $1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}8^{2} & ≡8 \\cdot 8≡64≡4 \\cdot 15 + 4≡4 & (mod15) \\\\ 8^{3} & ≡8 \\cdot 4≡32≡2 \\cdot 15 + 2≡2 & (mod15) \\\\ 8^{4} & ≡8 \\cdot 2≡16≡15 + 1≡1 & (mod15)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, $8 \\cdot 2 \\equiv 1 \\, (\\textrm{mod} \\:15)$. Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of $8$ modulo $15$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203704 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc98cf4e-55f1-5fbd-a779-fb400adf1b44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Inverses Using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm"}] :question/id #uuid "15204e14-a2a1-5591-8448-1864066a29b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-14658" :question/problem "Use the extended Euclidean algorithm to compute the multiplicative inverse of $15$ modulo $86$." :question/worked-solution "First of all, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(15,86)=1\n$$\n\nSo, the multiplicative inverse of $15$ modulo $86$ exists.\n\nThe multiplicative inverse of $15$ modulo $86$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n15\\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:86)\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $x$, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n86 &= \\begin{vmatrix}15 \\cdot 5 & +\\end{vmatrix}11 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 15 &= \\end{vmatrix}11 \\cdot 1\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 4 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 11|= & 4 \\cdot 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ ↙\\end{vmatrix}↙ \\\\\n4 &= \\begin{vmatrix}3 \\cdot 1 & +\\end{vmatrix}1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n11 &= 86 - 15 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n4 &= 15 - 11 \\\\\n3 &= 11 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n1 &= 4 - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 4 - 3 \\\\\n&= 4 - (11 - 4 \\cdot 2) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 3 - 11 \\\\\n&= (15 - 11) \\cdot 3 - 11 \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot 3 - 11 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot 3 - (86 - 15 \\cdot 5) \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot 23 - 86 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot 23 + 86 \\cdot (-4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result modulo $86$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\cdot 23 + 86 \\cdot (-4) & ≡1 & (mod86) \\\\ 15 \\cdot 23 & ≡1 & (mod86)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $15\\cdot 23\\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:86)$, which means that\n\n$$\n15^{-1} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 86=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23}\n$$"}]}
